# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Panic, Fail & Unreachable
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Aborts the program with `msg` (delegates to spark.panic). Never returns.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `msg`: the failure message. Borrowed.
##
## ## Retorno
## Nothing; the helper returns `void`. It aborts the program via `spark.panic`.
##
## ## Efectos
## The message passed to `spark.panic` is exactly `msg`.
##
## ## Memoria
## The message is borrowed and never freed; the process ends in `spark.panic`.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## calling ward.panic with "invalid state"
## ```
weave panic with msg as string into void:
    calling spark.panic with msg

## Aborts the program with `msg`. Ergonomic alias of panic.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `msg`: the failure message. Borrowed.
##
## ## Retorno
## Nothing; the helper returns `void`. It aborts the program via `spark.panic`.
##
## ## Efectos
## The message passed to `spark.panic` is exactly `msg`.
##
## ## Memoria
## The message is borrowed and never freed; the process ends in `spark.panic`.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## calling ward.fail with "invalid state"
## ```
weave fail with msg as string into void:
    calling spark.panic with msg

## Marks code that must never execute: panics with `msg` when reached.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `msg`: the failure message. Defaults to `internal error: unreachable code`.
##
## ## Retorno
## Nothing; the helper returns `void`. It aborts the program via `spark.panic`
## whenever it is called, because it is only meant to mark dead code.
##
## ## Efectos
## The message passed to `spark.panic` is exactly `msg`.
##
## ## Memoria
## The message is borrowed and never freed; the process ends in `spark.panic`.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## calling ward.unreachable with "unhandled judge branch"
## ```
weave unreachable with msg as string is "internal error: unreachable code" into void:
    calling spark.panic with msg

## Explicit alias of unreachable with failure prefix.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `msg`: the detail appended to the prefix. Defaults to `unreachable`.
##
## ## Retorno
## Nothing; the helper returns `void`. It aborts the program via `spark.panic`
## whenever it is called.
##
## ## Efectos
## The message passed to `spark.panic` is `internal error: <msg>`; the string is
## built by interpolation, so it is an owned temporary.
##
## ## Memoria
## The message is borrowed and never freed; the interpolated string is handed to
## `spark.panic`, which never returns.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## calling ward.fail_unreachable with "unhandled judge branch"
## ```
weave fail_unreachable with msg as string is "unreachable" into void:
    calling spark.panic with "internal error: {msg}"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Result-based Checks (Non-panicking)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Returns ok("ok") when `cond` is true, otherwise err(`msg`). Never panics.
## @deprecated Use `check_native` instead.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `cond`: the condition to check.
## - `msg`: the error payload used when `cond` is false. Borrowed.
##
## ## Retorno
## The legacy `oracle.ResultString` (owned by the caller): `ok("ok")` when `cond`
## holds, `err(msg)` otherwise.
##
## ## Memoria
## The returned `ResultString` owns its string field; release it with `banish`.
## The parameters are borrowed.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## let r as ResultString is calling ward.check with x > 0, "x must be positive"
## ```
weave check with cond as bool , msg as string into ResultString:
    if cond:
        return calling oracle.result_ok_string with "ok"
    return calling oracle.result_err_string with msg

## Returns ok("ok") when `a` equals `b`, otherwise err(`msg`). Never panics.
## @deprecated Use `check_eq_int_native` instead.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `a`: the actual value.
## - `b`: the expected value.
## - `msg`: the error payload used when the values differ. Borrowed.
##
## ## Retorno
## The legacy `oracle.ResultString` (owned by the caller): `ok("ok")` when
## `a == b`, `err(msg)` otherwise.
##
## ## Memoria
## The returned `ResultString` owns its string field; release it with `banish`.
## The parameters are borrowed.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## let r as ResultString is calling ward.check_eq_int with 2 + 2, 4, "bad sum"
## ```
weave check_eq_int with a as int , b as int , msg as string into ResultString:
    if a == b:
        return calling oracle.result_ok_string with "ok"
    return calling oracle.result_err_string with msg

